% Main program: includes the thin LE layer, which transitively includes the
% Prolog facts file (postcodes.le -> postcodes_layer.le -> postcodes_facts.pl).
% The rules below reason in pure LE on top of the Prolog-backed relations.

the knowledge base postcodes includes these resources:
    postcodes_layer.

the templates are:
    *a postcode* is Scottish.
    *a postcode* and *an other postcode* are in the same country.

the knowledge base postcodes includes:

a postcode is Scottish if
    the postcode is in the country scotland.

a first postcode and a second postcode are in the same country if
    the first postcode is in the country a country
    and the second postcode is in the country the country.

scenario test is:
    which_region expects answers ["eh1 is in edinburgh"].
    which_country expects answers ["eh1 is in the country scotland"].
    scots expects answers ["eh1 is Scottish", "eh8 is Scottish"].
    same_country expects answers ["ec1a and w1 are in the same country"].
    cross_country expects answers [].

% A postcode-backed lookup (Prolog fact via the thin layer):
query which_region is:
    eh1 is in which region.

% A two-hop lookup (postcode -> region -> country) through the Prolog helper:
query which_country is:
    eh1 is in the country which country.

% Pure-LE reasoning on top of the Prolog relation:
query scots is:
    which postcode is Scottish.

% Two specific London postcodes share a country (succeeds, no free variables):
query same_country is:
    ec1a and w1 are in the same country.

% A London and an Edinburgh postcode do NOT (expected: no answers):
query cross_country is:
    ec1a and eh1 are in the same country.
